<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════ §01 ══ -->
<section id="s-align" data-code="align">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Alignment Problem</div>
<h2>Speculative decoding data is fundamentally different from fine-tuning data</h2>

<p>When you fine-tune a language model, the training signal is straightforward: learn to predict the next token from a corpus of human text. The model's loss is well-defined, and adding more diverse high-quality text reliably helps. Speculative decoding training breaks this assumption in a critical way.</p>

<p>A draft model trained for speculative decoding has one job: predict what the <em>target model</em> would say next — not what a human would say, not what the ground-truth response is, but specifically what tokens the particular frozen autoregressive LLM you're accelerating would produce. The target model has its own style, its own vocabulary preferences, its own formatting quirks. Every time the draft predicts something the target wouldn't have said, that token gets rejected and the speculative step is wasted.</p>

<p>This creates the core challenge: <strong>your training corpus is not the target distribution.</strong> Public datasets like ShareGPT and UltraChat contain responses written by humans or generated by GPT-4 or other models. If you're accelerating Qwen3-8B, the draft needs to learn <em>Qwen3-8B's</em> response distribution, not a generic assistant's.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">distribution mismatch — draft vs. target token distributions</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag the KL slider to see the acceptance collapse</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="kl-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <div class="ctrl-row" style="margin-top:.875rem">
      <label>KL divergence</label>
      <input type="range" id="kl-slider" min="0" max="100" value="20" style="flex:1">
      <span class="val" id="kl-val">0.20</span>
      <span style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);margin-left:.5rem">avg accepted tokens / step: <strong id="kl-tau" style="color:var(--purple-l)">5.1</strong></span>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;margin-bottom:0">Left: target model's token distribution (purple). Right: draft model's distribution (cyan). Shaded overlap = probability of acceptance. As KL divergence grows — from training on misaligned data — the overlap shrinks and accepted tokens per step plummets.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The canonical speculative decoding acceptance rule (Leviathan et al., 2023) accepts a draft token t<sub>i</sub> with probability min(1, p<sub>target</sub>(t<sub>i</sub>) / p<sub>draft</sub>(t<sub>i</sub>)). In expectation, the number of accepted tokens per step is governed by the total variation distance between the two distributions — which is bounded below by half the KL divergence. Better training data → smaller KL divergence → more accepted tokens → higher speedup. This is not just a qualitative intuition; it is a provable relationship, and it makes the data pipeline as important as the model architecture.</p>

<h3>What "aligned" data looks like in practice</h3>
<p>For a chat model like Qwen3-8B-Instruct, aligned data means:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Prompts that match deployment queries</strong> — math, code, chat, instruction-following</li>
  <li><strong>Responses from the target model itself</strong> — not from humans or other LLMs</li>
  <li><strong>The same tokenizer, the same chat template</strong> — no template mismatch</li>
  <li><strong>Sufficient length</strong> — if you deploy with context lengths up to 8K tokens, train with sequences up to 8K tokens</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════ §02 ══ -->
<section id="s-theory" data-code="theory">
<div class="section-no">02 — Theory: How Data Determines Speedup</div>
<h2>The formula that connects training data quality to wall-clock speed</h2>

<p>Speculative decoding's speedup is fully characterized by three numbers. Understanding the formula is essential because it tells you exactly which data decisions move the needle.</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">speculative decoding speedup</div>
<span class="math-var">S</span> = <span class="math-var">τ</span> / (1 + <span class="math-var">γ</span> · <span class="math-var">c</span>)
<br>
<span class="math-comment">where:</span>
<br>
<span class="math-var">τ</span> = expected accepted tokens per step (1 ≤ τ ≤ γ+1)
<br>
<span class="math-var">γ</span> = speculation budget (number of draft tokens generated per step)
<br>
<span class="math-var">c</span> = draft model cost as fraction of target model cost
</div>

<p>The term <strong>γ·c</strong> in the denominator is fixed once you've chosen your draft model architecture — a 5-layer diffusion model like DFlash costs roughly 2–3% of a 32-layer target (c ≈ 0.023 for DFlash vs c ≈ 0.166 for EAGLE-3 at γ=16). The only variable that your training data can change is <strong>τ</strong>.</p>

<p>τ comes from a truncated geometric distribution. If each draft token is independently accepted with probability α, then:</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">expected acceptance length</div>
<span class="math-var">τ</span> = (1 − α<sup>γ+1</sup>) / (1 − α)
<br>
<span class="math-comment">approaches γ+1 as α → 1 (perfect draft), approaches 1 as α → 0 (random draft)</span>
</div>

<p>And α — the per-token acceptance probability — is bounded by the KL divergence between draft and target distributions. Good training data (same domain, target-generated responses) maximizes α. Poor training data (mismatched domain, human responses) suppresses α, collapses τ, and erases most of the speedup.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">interactive speedup formula — drag α to see how data quality maps to speedup</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag the acceptance rate slider</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="speedup-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <div class="ctrl-row" style="margin-top:.875rem">
      <label>acceptance rate α</label>
      <input type="range" id="alpha-slider" min="40" max="99" value="75" style="flex:1">
      <span class="val" id="alpha-val">0.75</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <label>speculation budget γ</label>
      <input type="range" id="gamma-slider" min="4" max="20" value="16" style="flex:1">
      <span class="val" id="gamma-val">16</span>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;margin-bottom:0">Purple curve: DFlash-style diffusion drafter (c=0.023). Cyan curve: EAGLE-style autoregressive drafter (c=0.166). The gap between curves shows why low drafting cost matters — but both curves collapse at low α. Training data controls where on the x-axis you land.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>A concrete example: the SpecForge team trained the same EAGLE-3 draft architecture on two corpora — ShareGPT+UltraChat (320K conversations) and Perfect-Blend (1.4M conversations of balanced chat/math/code). The result was τ = 2.82 vs τ = 3.48 on Math500 — purely from data quality and scale, with identical architecture. That 23% τ improvement translates to a 1.17× higher end-to-end speedup. With DFlash's lower c, the same τ gain is worth proportionally more.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════ §03 ══ -->
<section id="s-eras" data-code="eras">
<div class="section-no">03 — Three Eras of Data Strategy</div>
<h2>How draft model architectures changed what data you need</h2>

<p>The "right" data strategy is not universal — it depends on which draft model paradigm you're using. Each architectural generation has a different relationship to training data, and understanding the differences explains why some techniques (like response regeneration) matter more for recent methods.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="era-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" id="era-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Era 1 — Independent Small Model (2023)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">A separate smaller LLM (e.g., LLaMA-7B accelerating LLaMA-70B) is trained from scratch or fine-tuned. Data strategy: standard next-token prediction on any corpus. The model is trained to predict human text, not the target model. Alignment with the target is achieved structurally (same family, same tokenizer) rather than through data. Works but is memory-intensive and has a hard acceptance ceiling at ≈70–80% for in-distribution queries.</div>
      <div class="step-code">corpus = any_web_text + books  # alignment via architecture, not data</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="era-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Era 2 — Feature-Conditioned Adapters: EAGLE / Medusa (2024)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The draft model is conditioned on the frozen target model's hidden features. Now the draft is not predicting human text — it is predicting the target model's next tokens given the target model's internal representations. This creates a data alignment requirement: training samples should use target-model-generated responses so that the feature conditioning signal is consistent between training and inference. EAGLE's original paper claimed low data sensitivity, but this turned out to be an artifact of the feature prediction constraint (which EAGLE-3 later removed).</div>
      <div class="step-code">corpus = sharegpt + ultrachat  # 320K conversations, but limited math/code</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="era-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Era 3 — Training-Time Test + Scaled Data (EAGLE-3, DFlash, 2025–2026)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The draft model eliminates the feature prediction constraint (EAGLE-3) or adopts a diffusion formulation (DFlash). Both enable the acceptance length τ to scale proportionally with data. Response regeneration from the target model now has measurable impact (+5.3% throughput). Domain balance matters dramatically: a chat-only training corpus produces τ ≈ 2.8 on math benchmarks; a balanced corpus produces τ ≈ 6.5+. Scale matters: 1.4M conversations beats 320K by a consistent 17–23% in τ.</div>
      <div class="step-code">corpus = nemotron_v2 + codealpaca  # 800K samples, balanced domains, regenerated</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="era-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="era-next">Next →</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="era-prog">Era 1 of 3</span>
</div>

<h3>The architecture-data coupling</h3>
<p>The key insight from the EAGLE-3 paper is diagnostic: they discovered that EAGLE's original feature-prediction loss acted as a <em>bottleneck</em> that prevented the draft model from absorbing more training signal, causing τ to plateau at ≈100K samples regardless of how much data was added. By removing the feature prediction loss and adding Training-Time Test (simulating multi-step generation during training), EAGLE-3 restored the direct link between data scale and τ. DFlash achieves the same effect through its diffusion formulation: because the model predicts all block tokens in parallel from the target context, every additional sample adds genuine signal without saturation.</p>

<p>This means: <strong>if you're using an older EAGLE checkpoint, data scaling may not help much. If you're training EAGLE-3 or DFlash, data is your most powerful lever.</strong></p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════ §04 ══ -->
<section id="s-regen" data-code="regen">
<div class="section-no">04 — Response Regeneration</div>
<h2>The draft must predict the target, not humans</h2>

<p>The most impactful data decision for modern speculative decoding training is also the most counterintuitive: <em>throw away the dataset's provided responses and replace them with responses generated by the target model.</em></p>

<p>This is not optional polish — it is the primary mechanism by which training distribution aligns with inference behavior. At inference, the draft model sees: the prompt, plus tokens produced by the target model (the "bonus token" from the previous round). The draft's job is to predict what tokens come next — tokens that the target model would produce. If training used human-written responses, the draft learned to mimic human text; it never saw target-model outputs during training and is systematically misaligned.</p>

