<h3>How DFlash implements response regeneration</h3>
<p>DFlash's paper states directly: <em>"Instead of directly using the original dataset, we construct our training set with the responses generated by the target model for better target alignment."</em> The frozen target model processes every prompt in the Nemotron V2 + CodeAlpaca corpus and produces the training responses. These are then used as the ground-truth "next block" labels for the diffusion drafter to learn.</p>

<p>There is an important distinction in DFlash's training: because the target model also provides hidden features (via KV injection) that condition the draft at each layer, the training responses need to be consistent with what that conditioning signal "knows." Using target-model-generated responses ensures this consistency. A draft trained on human responses would be learning to predict text that the target model's conditioning features never pointed toward — a structurally misaligned setup.</p>

<h3>Practical implementation</h3>
<p>Response regeneration is computationally expensive — you need to run the full target model on every training prompt. There are two approaches:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Offline (pre-cache):</strong> Run the target model on all prompts before training, cache the responses. Also cache the hidden features if your architecture needs them. DFlash mentions this explicitly: offline training caches target hidden states for each token and loads them during draft optimization to reduce computational overhead during training.</li>
  <li><strong>Online (on-the-fly):</strong> Generate target-model outputs in real time during training. Simpler to implement, no disk overhead, but increases GPU memory pressure since both models must be loaded simultaneously.</li>
</ul>
<p>For datasets up to 1M samples, offline caching is strongly recommended. Hidden feature caching (for feature-conditioned architectures like EAGLE-3 and DFlash) trades disk space for training throughput; DFlash notes that 5 hidden feature layers per token are cached, requiring roughly 5× the disk space of storing just the responses.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════ §05 ══ -->
<section id="s-mix" data-code="mix">
<div class="section-no">05 — Corpus Design</div>
<h2>What domains to include and why scale matters</h2>

<p>Given that you've committed to using target-model-generated responses, the next decision is which prompts to use. The choice of domains — the subjects, task types, and styles in your corpus — directly determines which benchmarks your draft model excels on.</p>

<p>The pattern across papers is consistent: a chat-only training corpus (ShareGPT, UltraChat) leaves substantial performance on the table for math and code benchmarks, while a balanced corpus covering all three domains achieves near-optimal τ across the board. The quantitative gap is not subtle:</p>

<div class="results-bars" id="mix-bars">
  <div class="bar-section-head">Acceptance length τ on Math500 — EAGLE-3 / LLaMA-3.1-8B</div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">ShareGPT + UltraChat (320K)</div>
