</div>

<p>The top three bars compare data mix choices at fixed architecture (EAGLE-3 on LLaMA-3.1-8B). ShareGPT+UltraChat achieves τ = 2.82; the balanced Perfect-Blend corpus more than doubles the acceptance length to 3.48. The bottom bars show DFlash's per-domain variation — math achieves the highest τ (8.01) because mathematical reasoning follows highly predictable token patterns (step-by-step arithmetic, LaTeX notation). Chat is lowest even with DFlash's conditioning advantage.</p>

<h3>How to choose your domain mix</h3>
<p>The right corpus composition depends entirely on your deployment scenario. A practical decision framework:</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Deployment scenario</th><th>Recommended corpus</th><th>Domains to prioritize</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>General chat assistant</td><td>UltraChat, WildChat, OpenHermes</td><td>Multi-turn conversation, instruction-following</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Math / reasoning</td><td>NuminaMath, OpenMathInstruct, Nemotron STEM</td><td>Math reasoning with CoT traces</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Code assistant</td><td>CodeAlpaca, Magicoder, LiveCodeBench prompts</td><td>Code generation, debugging, unit tests</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Production (balanced)</strong></td><td class="mn"><strong>Perfect-Blend / Nemotron V2 + CodeAlpaca</strong></td><td><strong>All three — with domain weights tuned to query mix</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>DFlash's choice of NVIDIA Nemotron Post-Training Dataset V2 + CodeAlpaca is well-reasoned for a general-purpose speculative decoder. Nemotron V2 contains 6.3M samples spanning math (239K), STEM (355K), code (175K), chat (628K), and multilingual content — all synthetically generated (by DeepSeek-R1-0528, Qwen variants, etc.). CodeAlpaca adds 20K code-specific instruction pairs. Together they provide broad domain coverage without over-representing any single domain, and at 800K samples total (after subsampling) they are large enough to avoid τ saturation under DFlash's architecture.</p>

<h3>The 1M sample sweet spot</h3>
<p>Empirically, τ scales roughly log-linearly with data for modern architectures (EAGLE-3, DFlash) up to approximately 1–2M samples, after which returns diminish. Under the original EAGLE architecture, this scaling was broken by the feature prediction constraint — τ plateaued at ~100K samples regardless of more data. EAGLE-3's training-time test and DFlash's diffusion formulation both restore log-linear scaling. A practical starting point for production training is 500K–1.5M samples with balanced domain coverage.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════ §06 ══ -->
<section id="s-anchor" data-code="anchor">
<div class="section-no">06 — Anchor Sampling</div>
<h2>DFlash's technique for getting more training signal from the same corpus</h2>

<p>Once you have your regenerated, domain-balanced corpus, there is one more data preparation technique that DFlash introduces specifically for block-diffusion training: <strong>anchor sampling</strong>. This is a training-time augmentation that extracts multiple independent training examples from a single sequence — dramatically increasing the effective dataset size without adding new prompts.</p>

<h3>The problem: standard block construction wastes context</h3>
<p>In standard block diffusion training, you divide the response into fixed consecutive blocks (e.g., tokens 1–16, tokens 17–32, etc.) and mask random positions within each block. This is deterministic — a 512-token response always produces the same 32 training blocks in the same positions. The model learns to predict each block conditioned on the preceding clean tokens, but it always sees the same "anchor" structure.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">anchor sampling animation — one sequence → many overlapping training blocks</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Play to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="anchor-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.625rem;margin-top:.75rem">
      <button id="anchor-play" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">▶ Play</button>
      <button id="anchor-reset" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">↺ Reset</button>
      <span style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-left:.5rem;align-self:center">Epoch <span id="anchor-epoch">1</span> / 6</span>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0">Yellow squares = anchor tokens (clean, visible to the model). Green = mask tokens (the model must predict these). Each epoch draws new random anchor positions from the same sequence — the same 512 tokens produce 512 independent training problems across epochs. Without anchor sampling, each position appears as a training target at most once per sequence.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>How anchor sampling works</h3>
<p>DFlash's training procedure draws a set of <strong>anchor tokens</strong> — randomly sampled from the clean response — for each sequence at every epoch. Anchors serve as the "start" of each mask block: they are clean tokens that the model conditions on, and the next (block_size − 1) tokens are masked and must be predicted. By randomizing anchor positions:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The same prompt+response pair produces a different training signal each epoch</li>
  <li>The draft model sees more diverse context-prediction combinations</li>
  <li>Coverage is better: every token in the response eventually appears as both an anchor (context) and as a masked target across epochs</li>
</ul>
<p>DFlash samples 512 anchor positions per sequence per epoch (with max sequence length 3072 tokens), meaning each training sequence produces 512 independent masked block prediction tasks. Over 6 training epochs, that's effectively 3072 distinct learning signals per original training example — an order of magnitude more supervision than standard fixed-block training.</p>

<h3>Measured impact of anchor sampling</h3>
<p>DFlash's ablation study directly compares standard block construction vs. random anchor sampling on 100K training samples. The improvement is substantial across all benchmarks:</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Block construction</th><th>Math500 τ</th><th>HumanEval τ</th><th>MT-Bench τ</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Standard (fixed consecutive blocks)</td><td class="mn">4.69</td><td class="mn">3.90</td><td class="mn">3.07</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>Random anchor sampling</td><td class="mn best">5.64</td><td class="mn best">4.61</td><td class="mn best">3.18</td></tr>
    <tr><td><em>Improvement</em></td><td class="mn" style="color:var(--green)">+20.3%</td><td class="mn" style="color:var(--green)">+18.2%</td><td class="mn" style="color:var(--green)">+3.6%</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The improvement is largest on math benchmarks (where block structure matters most) and smallest on chat (where token patterns are more uniform regardless of anchor placement). The effect is data-augmentation at the presentation layer — you get more effective training samples from the same corpus without any additional data collection or target-model inference.</p>

<h3>Loss weighting: emphasize early positions</h3>
<p>DFlash introduces one more training data technique that interacts with anchor sampling: <strong>position-dependent loss weighting</strong>. Within each masked block, earlier positions receive higher loss weight. The intuition: in speculative decoding, an error at the first draft token invalidates all subsequent tokens in that round. Errors at later positions waste less. DFlash uses an exponentially decaying weight for token at position k within a block:</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">position-dependent loss weight</div>
w<sub>k</sub> = exp(−(k−1) / <span class="math-var">γ</span>)
<br>
<span class="math-comment">where γ controls the decay rate (set to 7 for block size 16)</span>
<br>
<span class="math-comment">effect: DFlash's loss convergence is ~15% faster, final acceptance length ~4% higher vs uniform weighting</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════ §07 ══ -->
<section id="s-recipe" data-code="recipe">
<div class="section-no">07 — Production Recipe</div>
<h2>A step-by-step checklist for building your own training corpus</h2>

<p>Putting the preceding sections together, here is a concrete decision pipeline for building a production-quality training corpus for a speculative decoding draft model. The steps below target an EAGLE-3 or DFlash-style drafter with a feature-conditioned architecture.</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th><th>Key decision</th><th>Impact on τ</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">1</td>
      <td>Collect raw prompts</td>
      <td>Choose source datasets matching your deployment domain mix (math/code/chat)</td>
      <td>Determines domain coverage</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">2</td>
      <td>Run target model on all prompts</td>
      <td>Temperature: 0 (greedy) for max alignment; 0.8 for diversity. DFlash: greedy.</td>
      <td><strong>+5–19% vs original responses</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">3</td>
      <td>Cache hidden features (offline training)</td>
      <td>Extract from 5 uniformly-sampled layers between layer 2 and layer N-3</td>
      <td>Enables efficient training (no on-the-fly target inference)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">4</td>
      <td>Filter by length</td>
      <td>Keep sequences 128–3072 tokens (4096 for long-context models). Remove very short sequences (&#60; 64 tokens).</td>
      <td>Short sequences train short-context prediction; deployment mismatch hurts τ</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">5</td>
      <td>Balance domain weights</td>
      <td>If overrepresented: subsample. If underrepresented: upsample. DFlash: Nemotron 97.5% + CodeAlpaca 2.5%.</td>
      <td>+23% on underrepresented domains</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">6</td>
      <td>Configure anchor sampling</td>
      <td>Sample 512 random anchors per 3072-token sequence per epoch. Use 6 epochs total.</td>
      <td><strong>+18–20% on math/code</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row">
      <td class="mn">7</td>
      <td>Apply loss weighting</td>
      <td>Exponential decay w_k = exp(−(k−1)/γ) with γ = 7 for block size 16</td>
      <td>~4% additional τ gain + faster convergence</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Quick implementation guide</h3>
<p>For EAGLE-3-style training on a 8B target model (single H100 80GB), a practical working configuration:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Corpus scale:</strong> 800K–1.5M conversations (regenerated from target model)</li>
  <li><strong>Domain mix:</strong> ≈40% chat, ≈35% math/STEM, ≈25% code (adjust to deployment)</li>
  <li><strong>Sequence length:</strong> max 3072 tokens for instruct models; 4096 for coding/reasoning</li>
  <li><strong>Training:</strong> 6 epochs, AdamW lr=6×10⁻⁴, cosine schedule, warmup ratio 0.04</li>
  <li><strong>TTT length:</strong> 7 steps for general; 13 steps for math/code-heavy deployment (SpecForge finding)</li>
  <li><strong>Verification:</strong> Track τ separately on Math500, HumanEval, MT-Bench; all three should improve together if your domain balance is right</li>
</ul>

<p>If you see τ on math benchmarks stuck below 3.0 while chat τ is above 4.0, you need more math-domain training data. If τ is uniformly low across all benchmarks, the most likely culprit is response regeneration — verify that your training responses were generated by the target model, not copied from the raw dataset.</p>

<h3>What DFlash's 6× speedup really cost in data</h3>
<p>DFlash trained on ≈800K regenerated samples across Nemotron V2 + CodeAlpaca, for 6 epochs with 512 anchor positions per sequence. That's approximately 2.4 billion (800K × 6 epochs × 512 anchors) independent masked block prediction problems — from a corpus that could fit in a single download. The training cost is dominated by running the frozen target model to extract hidden features and generate responses: roughly 800K × 3072 tokens of target-model inference, comparable to fine-tuning for 2–3B tokens. The result: τ = 8.01 on Math500 and 6.63 on HumanEval — the highest acceptance lengths published for a sub-7B drafter at the time of writing.</p>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display: none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Building Training Data for Speculative Decoding Drafters</h2>
    <p>Speculative decoding training data is fundamentally different from standard fine-tuning data. The draft model must predict what the target model would say — not humans. This means the single most important data decision is response regeneration: replacing dataset responses with outputs from the frozen target model. SpecForge quantified this at plus five-point-three percent throughput improvement on average.</p>
    <p>Domain balance is the second lever. Training on ShareGPT and UltraChat alone produces an acceptance length of 2.82 on math benchmarks. A balanced corpus of 1.4 million conversations covering math, code, and chat raises this to 3.48 — a 23 percent improvement with identical architecture. DFlash compounds these gains with anchor sampling, randomly drawing 512 anchor positions from each training sequence each epoch, achieving 18 to 20 percent additional acceptance length improvement on math and code. Together these techniques explain how DFlash reaches tau equals 8.01 on Math500 — a figure that comes from the data pipeline, not just the model architecture.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：为投机解码起草模型构建训练数据</h2>
    <p>投机解码训练数据与标准微调数据存在根本性差异。草稿模型必须预测目标模型的输出，而非人类书写的文本。因此，最关键的数据决策是响应重新生成：用冻结目标模型生成的输出替换数据集中的原始响应。SpecForge量化了这一效果：平均吞吐量提升5.3%。</p>
    <p>领域均衡是第二个关键杠杆。仅使用ShareGPT和UltraChat训练，在数学基准上的接受长度τ仅为2.82。而包含数学、代码和对话的均衡语料库（140万对话）可将其提升至3.48，在架构完全相同的情况下提升了23%。DFlash通过锚点采样进一步放大收益，每轮训练从每个序列中随机抽取512个锚点位置，在数学和代码任务上额外提升了18%至20%的接受长度。这些技术共同解释了DFlash如何在Math500上达到τ等于8.01——这一数字源于数据流水线，而非单纯的模型架构。</p>
  </div>
</div>

